name: nochmal-lessons
description: Create, curate, audit, and release numbered Nochmal A2 lessons from the existing blueprint and authored course records. Use when the user names A2 lesson numbers or asks for the lesson pipeline, audio, publication, or deployment.
---

# Numbered A2 lesson workflow

The user supplies lesson numbers. `curriculum/a2_blueprint.md` is existing,
read-only planning input. A1 is already published. Do not choose a new topic,
rewrite the blueprint, or alter published lessons while creating or auditing.
B1 is outside this workflow until the user asks for it.

Before creating or substantially editing a lesson, read
`docs/grammar-breakdown-rulebook.md` and `docs/lesson-format.md` in full. Their
publication checklist is a release gate. Preserve staged curriculum records;
only the publication manifests expose lessons in the app.

## Create or curate requested numbers

1. Read each numbered blueprint entry and its matching record in
   `curriculum/a2_course/lessons/`. These authored records already contain
   passage text and annotations for A2 lessons 001–072. Do not overwrite one
   simply to make a candidate.
2. Run `.venv/bin/python scripts/lesson_flow.py status a2-course-NNN` and
   `prepare` for a requested unpublished number. `prepare` copies an existing
   authored record only when its candidate is absent, then compiles it.
3. Curate the candidate sentence by sentence: exact source tokens, word cards,
   useful phrase results, controlled tags, visible changes, complete verb and
   inflection help, natural translations, and blueprint fit. Put contextual
   corrections in structured lesson metadata; add reusable grammar logic only
   for a genuinely general unsupported form. Never put a whole sentence branch
   in shared code. If the German or English passage changes, update the
   matching authored A2 record as well so subsequent audio generation uses
   the exact same text. Keep the blueprint unchanged.
4. Run the candidate build and relevant tests. Leave the app and publication
   manifests untouched. Report the file paths and remaining issues.

## Audit and repair

When the user asks Codex to audit a lesson, audit and repair it in the same
task. Do not stop after reporting findings. Keep ordinary candidate fixes
focused on lesson-source metadata and rebuild once after editing.

For command-driven repair, use
`.venv/bin/python scripts/lesson_flow.py audit-fix a2-course-NNN`. By default
it uses one Terra Medium session to review, repair the complete unpublished
lesson, rebuild, and record a final verdict bound to the candidate source hash.
It may correct candidate metadata, rewrite the passage to meet the blueprint,
and synchronize the matching authored source. It may add reusable shared
grammar logic and focused tests when required. It never changes the blueprint,
published lessons, audio, or deployment state. Use `--strict --model gpt-6.1-sol
--reasoning-effort high` only when an independent Sol High audit, repair, and
repeat audit loop is required; it can use several model sessions. Never
hand-write a passing review report to bypass this step.

If you are the read-only reviewer launched by `lesson_flow.py audit`, perform
the audit directly. Do not launch another agent or call `lesson_flow.py audit`
again. Ignore any earlier `review.json` verdict when judging the content.

## Audio and release

When the user asks for audio and publication, run `audio` for passing audited
lessons. It checks that the candidate passage matches the authored A2 course
record, generates or reuses ElevenLabs audio, attaches it, and checks the
review again. Audio generation may consume ElevenLabs credits.

Run `preview`, then inspect the lesson in the running app at desktop and mobile
widths, including the audio, before running `inspect`. The inspection command
requires `--desktop-reviewed --mobile-reviewed --audio-reviewed` and records
hashes of what was inspected; never run it without actually looking.
Run the complete unit suite and `publish` only after all release checklist
items pass. `publish` is the explicit change to the app's published-lesson list.

For mobile release, run `upload` for published audio, then `mobile-prepare`.
Inspect the preview at desktop and mobile widths, then run `mobile-inspect`,
`mobile-install`, and `deploy`. The
last step changes the live Cloudflare Pages site. A request to create or audit
lessons does not authorize these release actions; a request to release the
named lessons does.

Use `docs/lesson-workflow.md` for the exact commands and file locations. Stop
at the first failed gate, report the blocking lesson and evidence, and preserve
all other authored and staged records.
# Nochmal grammar breakdown rulebook

This is the mandatory, lesson-independent contract for integrating learning
content into Nochmal. Read it together with [lesson-format.md](lesson-format.md)
before changing a lesson.

The purpose of this document is consistency. Every lesson uses the same data
types, card types, hover layouts, validation rules, and publication process.
Lesson-specific grammar essays are not part of the product.

## 1. Product rule

The learning interface contains:

1. the German passage and audio;
2. sentence-level German and natural English;
3. ordered word cards;
4. ordered phrase-result cards;
5. controlled grammar tags;
6. fixed-layout reference hovers; and
7. speaking practice when the lesson has reviewed audio.

Do not add a “Lesson goals”, “Grammar explanation”, “What to notice”, or
similar lesson-description panel. Grammar belongs beside the exact word or
phrase where the learner encounters it.

Internal curriculum-planning fields may remain in the source corpus, but they
must not generate learner-facing prose.

## 2. Typed content contract

Production lesson content is validated with Pydantic. Use the existing models;
do not pass unstructured dictionaries into the renderer.

| Model | One responsibility |
|---|---|
| `Lesson` | identity, sequence, titles, sentences, audio and practice data |
| `Sentence` | exact German, natural English, audio and breakdown groups |
| `SentenceAnnotations` | sentence-owned tags, base forms, contextual word meanings, audited phrase/tag exclusions and authored phrases |
| `BreakdownGroup` | one ordered collection of learning cards |
| `BreakdownItem` | one word card or one phrase-result card |
| `GrammarTag` | one controlled label and category |
| `VerbHelp` | the fixed verb-reference data |
| `InflectionHelp` | the fixed case/declension-reference data |
| `SpeechAlternative` | one deliberately accepted speaking alternative |
| `PerformanceActivity` | one typed retrieval task with concrete prompts |

### 2.1 No free-form hover fields

A lesson cannot author tooltip prose. It supplies controlled fields, and the
shared renderer supplies the wording and layout.

`InflectionHelp` contains only:

- exact surface form;
- base form;
- controlled kind;
- controlled paradigm;
- case;
- gender when applicable;
- number;
- pronoun slot when applicable;
- resolved article form for a contraction; and
- contraction expansion when applicable.

Pydantic must reject incompatible combinations, such as a definite article
using an adjective paradigm or a plural slot marked singular.

### 2.2 One source of truth

Keep each fact in one place:

- curriculum JSON: passage text, translation, sequence, audio paths,
  `SentenceAnnotations` inputs (including concise contextual word meanings
  and numbered context for repeated surface forms, including contextual verb senses)
  and deliberate speaking alternatives;
- Pydantic models: allowed fields and combinations;
- `grammar_reference.py`: complete reusable paradigms;
- `models.py`: controlled grammar tags and fixed definitions;
- renderer: shared HTML structure;
- this rulebook: authoring and publication policy.

Never add a full sentence as a special branch in shared rendering or grammar
reference code. Sentence-specific analysis is content data; paradigms and
layouts are shared code.

## 3. Required sentence audit

Before building cards, audit every sentence from left to right.

| Surface | Base | Class | Role | Case/tense | Trigger | Visible change | Card/help |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| exact token or phrase | reusable form | controlled class | contextual role | exact value | governing construction | exact form change | word, phrase, verb or inflection |

Check every:

- noun and noun gender;
- singular or plural form;
- article and determiner;
- adjective and whether it is attributive, predicate or adverbial;
- pronoun and contextual case;
- preposition and governed case;
- finite verb, infinitive, participle, prefix and tense;
- contraction;
- clause boundary and finite-verb position;
- conjunction, adverb and particle; and
- fixed expression whose meaning is not compositional.

The audit is complete only when every source token has a word card and every
meaningful transformation has the required structured help.

## 4. Word-card contract

A word card always represents a reusable item.

### Nouns

- Use the dictionary article: `der`, `die` or `das`.
- Use the singular dictionary form unless the noun is plural-only.
- Keep noun gender separate from sentence case.
- Do not infer feminine gender from plural `die`.

### Verbs

- Use the correct lexical infinitive.
- Treat separability and reflexivity as lexical facts.
- Use the contextual English meaning on the visible word card.
- Attach `VerbHelp` to every verb card.

### Adjectives

- Store the reusable uninflected form.
- Put the visible declined form in `source_forms`.
- Mark attributive adjectives as adjectives.
- Do not give predicate or adverbial adjectives a declension card.

### Function words

- Classify the word by its actual sentence function.
- A preposition is not a separated prefix.
- A sentence-initial adverb is not automatically a conjunction.
- A capitalized token is not automatically a noun.

## 5. Phrase-result contract

A phrase card shows what selected word cards produce together.

A valid phrase:

- uses exact source wording;
- has a natural contextual English meaning;
- contains a useful learning unit;
- appears after its component word cards;
- has only controlled tags;
- carries the contextual case tag when it contains case morphology; and
- carries typed `InflectionHelp` for each inspectable case form.

Do not create:

- a second bare article+noun card that merely repeats the noun card;
- a whole-clause case tag because one noun phrase inside it has a case;
- a phrase containing words absent from the source;
- a phrase that crosses an unrelated noun or clause boundary; or
- a free-form explanation disguised as a phrase.

## 6. Fixed hover layouts

All hovers are renderer-owned. Lessons select content through typed fields;
they never select layout or wording.

### 6.1 Grammar-tag hover

Every tag hover has exactly:

1. tag label;
2. tag category; and
3. the fixed definition from `TAG_DESCRIPTIONS`.

The lesson cannot override the definition.

### 6.2 Verb hover

Every verb hover has exactly:

1. **Dictionary form** — infinitive and English meaning;
2. **Simple present** — a six-row bilingual table;
3. **Form in this sentence** — visible German and contextual English; and
4. a complete table for another relevant tense when applicable.

No lesson-authored usage paragraph is displayed in the hover.

### 6.3 Case and declension hover

Every case-bearing surface form uses the same reference-card shell:

1. reference type;
2. base form;
3. sentence form;
4. contraction expansion when applicable;
5. case, gender/person and number chips;
6. paradigm name; and
7. the complete paradigm table with the current cell highlighted.

The table is mandatory. A bare statement such as `der → dem` or
`klein → kleinen` is not sufficient.

Supported shared tables are:

- definite articles;
- indefinite articles;
- possessive determiners;
- der-word determiners such as `jeder`;
- negative determiners such as `kein`;
- weak adjective endings;
- mixed adjective endings;
- strong adjective endings; and
- personal pronouns.

If a lesson needs a paradigm that the typed model does not support, extend the
shared model, reference table, renderer and tests before publishing the lesson.

## 7. Case analysis

Determine the grammatical trigger first. Never infer case only from an ending.

### Nominative

Use for a subject or a relevant predicate-noun construction.

### Accusative

Common triggers are a direct object, an accusative preposition, or a two-way
preposition expressing a destination or placement.

### Dative

Common triggers are a dative preposition, recipient/complement, or a two-way
preposition expressing a fixed location.

### Genitive

Use for genuine possession or a genitive-governing construction. Do not call a
feminine `-er` form genitive unless the phrase function establishes genitive.

### Two-way prepositions

Choose by the phrase’s meaning:

- fixed location / **where?** → normally dative;
- destination or placement / **where to?** → normally accusative.

Do not use the unreliable shortcut “movement means accusative.”

### Invisible case

A proper place name may carry governed case without displaying an ending. Tag
the governing preposition and phrase, but do not invent an inflection card when
no visible form exists.

## 8. Inflection rules
# Nochmal lesson format

This file defines the production data and visual format used by every lesson.
The pedagogical audit and release gate are in
[grammar-breakdown-rulebook.md](grammar-breakdown-rulebook.md).

## Fixed lesson page

The mobile lesson page is rendered in this order:

1. course and lesson selectors;
2. first listen with the complete audio;
3. complete German paragraph and full audio; and
4. sentence-by-sentence breakdown with replay and reference cards.

There is no lesson-specific “goals and grammar” panel. Explanations appear only
on the word, phrase or form they describe.

## Fixed sentence card

Each sentence card contains:

1. sentence number;
2. sentence audio when available;
3. exact German;
4. natural English;
5. ordered word cards; and
6. phrase-result cards positioned after their inputs.

Cards follow German source order. A phrase may appear after its final component
even when that produces a small nested flow.

## Pydantic object map

| Object | Required shape |
|---|---|
| `Sentence` | `text`, `natural_meaning`, `breakdown_groups`, `audio_path` |
| `SentenceAnnotations` | controlled `word_tags`, numbered `word_occurrences` for repeated surfaces, `base_forms`, contextual `word_meanings` and `verb_meanings`, explicit ambiguous `verb_forms`, discontinuous `verb_components`, numeral `spoken_forms`, audited phrase/tag exclusions, and authored `phrases` |
| `BreakdownGroup` | ordered `items` |
| `BreakdownItem` | `german`, natural/contextual `english`, optional numeral `spoken_form`, optional `literal_meaning`, `kind`, `tags`, `source_forms`, optional typed help |
| `GrammarTag` | controlled `label` and matching `category` |
| `VerbHelp` | infinitive, meaning, tense and complete conjugation data |
| `InflectionHelp` | surface, base, kind, paradigm, case and grammatical slot |
| `PerformanceActivity` | controlled task kind, actionable instruction and concrete prompts |

Use `kind="word"` for reusable vocabulary and `kind="phrase"` for a
composed result.

## Performance activities

When the blueprint defines an additional retrieval task, store it as typed
`performance_activity` metadata with its controlled `kind`, one actionable
instruction, and any concrete prompts or cards needed to perform it. Candidate
previews expose this metadata for audit before audio and release.

## Word items

### Nouns

- German: dictionary article plus dictionary noun.
- English: noun meaning without an unnecessary English article.
- Tags: noun, gender, and plural when applicable.
- `source_forms`: every exact sentence token represented by the card.

### Verbs

- German: exact sentence form on the visible card.
- English: exact contextual meaning.
- Tags: only controlled verb/tense facts.
- `verb_help`: mandatory complete `VerbHelp`.
- `verb_components`: map the finite or nonfinite surface to any separated source
  tokens owned by the same verb card, such as `{"tut": ["leid"]}`.

### Adjectives

- German: uninflected base.
- English: contextual meaning.
- Tags: `adjective` when used adjectivally.
- `source_forms`: declined surface form when different.

### Other words

Use the reusable form and contextual English meaning. Add only controlled tags
that are true in the current sentence.

When a reusable dictionary gloss would be misleading for the exact surface in
context, author the concise visible meaning in `SentenceAnnotations.word_meanings`.
This field changes only the word card's contextual English; it does not replace
the shared dictionary form, verb table, or grammar metadata.

When the current sentence uses a different verb sense from the shared dictionary
entry, author that sense in `SentenceAnnotations.verb_meanings`. It updates only
the bilingual rows for the current sentence form; the shared dictionary meaning
and simple-present reference remain reusable.

When coordination or punctuation makes a verb form structurally ambiguous, use
`SentenceAnnotations.verb_forms` to mark the exact surface as an infinitive.
Do not use it to override an unambiguous form.

When the same surface occurs more than once with different grammar or meaning,
use a numbered `word_occurrences` entry. Its `surface` and one-based
`occurrence` select exactly one token; it may supply controlled tags, tag
exclusions, a base form, a meaning, or a spoken form. Do not apply one
surface-wide annotation to occurrences with different number, case, or sense.

For a numeric source form pronounced digit by digit, add its German pronunciation
to `SentenceAnnotations.spoken_forms`. The numeral card keeps the written source
form, displays the German spoken form, and gives its English digit meaning.

## Phrase items

A phrase item:

- uses exact German source tokens;
- supplies one natural contextual English meaning;
- supplies `literal_meaning` when a natural translation hides structure that is useful to the learner;
- adds structural value beyond its word cards;
- has controlled tags; and
- contains `inflection_help` when it exposes case morphology.

Useful phrase types include noun phrases, prepositional phrases, contractions,
fixed expressions, question order, inversion, separated verbs, modal
constructions, perfect-tense brackets and subordinate clauses.

Do not create a phrase merely to repeat an article+noun dictionary card.

## Grammar tags

Tags are controlled metadata, never prose.

- Labels must exist in `TAG_KEYWORDS`.
- Categories must match `TAG_KEYWORDS`.
- Definitions come only from `TAG_DESCRIPTIONS`.
- The tag hover always shows label, category and fixed definition.
- Lesson data cannot add or override a definition.
- One pill represents one fact.
- Do not combine case and a redundant object-role tag.

## Verb reference hover

The renderer always uses this structure:

```text
Dictionary form
  infinitive | English

Simple present
  pronoun | German | English
  ... six rows

Form in this sentence
  surface | contextual English

Relevant tense (when not present)
  pronoun | German | English
```

Every verb uses the same sections and column order.

## Inflection reference hover

The renderer always uses this structure:

```text
Reference type

Base form | Sentence form | Contraction (when present)

case | gender/person | number

Paradigm name
  case | complete paradigm columns
  ... current cell highlighted
```

Allowed `kind` and `paradigm` combinations are enforced by
`InflectionHelp`:

| kind | allowed paradigm |
|---|---|
| definite article | definite |
| indefinite article | indefinite |
| negative determiner (`kein`) | negative |
| possessive determiner | possessive |
| der-word determiner | der-word |
| adjective | weak, mixed or strong adjective |
| personal pronoun | personal pronoun |

The full tables come from `grammar_reference.py`; lesson data never stores a
partial table or explanatory sentence.

## Source-form linkage

`source_forms` connects a reusable card to exact source text:

- noun dictionary form to a declined/plural source;
- adjective base to declined adjective;
- preposition base to a contraction;
- verb card to all source tokens in a discontinuous construction.

Every source token must be represented exactly once among word-card source
forms. Phrase cards reuse those tokens as composed results.

## Shared-code boundary

Shared code may contain:

- controlled vocabularies;
- complete paradigms;
- reusable matching and validation;
- fixed rendering functions; and
- accessibility behavior.

Shared code must not contain:

- a branch matching one full lesson sentence;
- a custom tooltip sentence for one lesson;
- a special layout for one grammar point; or
- generated lesson-description prose.

Sentence-specific tags, base-form corrections, contextual word meanings,
audited exclusions of invalid automatic word or phrase tags, and extra phrases belong in the
sentence's `annotations` object in curriculum JSON. The compiler consumes that
same schema for every lesson; it must not keep a parallel sentence lookup
table in Python.

When context cannot be determined safely, add structured lesson metadata or
extend the model. Do not guess.

## Accessibility

Every hover target must:

- support pointer hover and keyboard focus;
- provide a useful aria label;
- keep the reference card inside the viewport;
- support vertical scrolling;
- allow wide tables to scroll horizontally on narrow screens; and
- use the same content for hover and focus.

## Required tests

Before publishing a lesson, test:

- Pydantic rejects invalid model combinations;
- every source token is represented;
- every verb has complete bilingual tables;
- every case-bearing phrase has the required typed help;
- each help surface exists inside its phrase;
- the help case matches the phrase case tag;
- every reference table has all four cases;
- the current table cell is highlighted;
- no lesson-description panel is rendered; and
- the complete lesson-library validator passes.

Run:

```bash
.venv/bin/python scripts/lesson_flow.py preflight LESSON_ID
```

